==================================================
NAMESPACE
==================================================

The application already knows the relevant application context and namespace information.

Do not build an unnecessary cluster-wide namespace discovery mechanism.

The UI should provide a namespace selector appropriate to the existing application context.

Conceptually:

Namespace: [ namespace ▼ ]    [ Discover ]

Once the user selects/finalizes the namespace and clicks Discover:
- discover pods
- retrieve the relevant pod snapshot information
- display the table

Inspect the existing application/namespace logic before designing the API.

Reuse the existing application context and Kubernetes session infrastructure.

==================================================
POD DISCOVERY
==================================================

The new Kubernetes Log Streamer should discover ALL relevant pods in the selected namespace.

Do not filter only to Running pods.

The backend should determine, for each pod/container:
- whether current logs are available
- whether previous container logs are available
- whether streaming is possible
- what containers exist
- whether sidecars exist

The frontend should render the appropriate state.

Do not make the frontend infer Kubernetes log availability merely from pod status.

==================================================
CONTAINERS AND SIDECARS
==================================================

A pod may contain:
- one application container
- multiple application containers
- sidecar containers

Do not blindly merge all container logs into one stream.

The design should support container selection.

For a single-container pod:

[ Stream ]

For a multi-container pod, the user should be able to choose the container.

Example:

Container:
[ application ▼ ]
    application
    sidecar

The UI should clearly identify:
- Pod
- Container
- Current container
- Previous container where available

Use Kubernetes terminology correctly.

Do NOT call previous container logs "previous pod logs".

A container restart does not necessarily mean the pod was replaced.

==================================================
HISTORICAL LOGS
==================================================

When Stream is clicked:

1. Create a session
2. Create a temporary disk-backed workspace
3. Retrieve available/recent historical logs using Kubernetes
4. Write them to the session workspace
5. Display them in the existing-style log viewer
6. Do NOT start follow/live streaming automatically

The initial historical retrieval must have a sensible/configurable limit.

Do not assume Kubernetes can provide unlimited historical pod logs.

The implementation should use the Kubernetes log API/client appropriately.

Investigate and document:
- tail_lines
- timestamps
- since_seconds / since_time where appropriate
- previous
- follow

Determine the best initial historical retrieval strategy from the repository and actual requirements.

==================================================
DISK-BACKED SESSION MODEL
==================================================

Do NOT keep the complete log stream in Python process memory.

This utility may be used by 10–20+ developers concurrently and logs can be large.

Use temporary disk-backed session workspaces.

Conceptually:

/temporary/log/session/
    session-<id>/
        metadata.json
        logs/
            application.log
            sidecar.log

The exact directory should follow existing repository/configuration conventions.

Each session must be isolated by session ID/user/session ownership.

The logs are temporary operational data only.

They must NOT become persistent application data.

==================================================
SESSION LIFECYCLE
==================================================

Session creation:

Stream
  ↓
Create session
  ↓
Create temporary workspace
  ↓
Load historical logs
  ↓
Display static logs

Stop Live:

- close the live Kubernetes connection
- preserve the session
- preserve the temporary log files
- allow the user to continue reading/filtering historical logs

Terminate Session:

- stop live Kubernetes streams
- close WebSocket connections
- delete session state
- delete all temporary log files
- delete the complete temporary workspace
- clear the UI/session

After termination, nothing from that log session should remain on disk.

==================================================
CONCURRENT SESSIONS
==================================================

Support multiple users and multiple independent log sessions.

Different users may:
- view the same pod
- view different pods
- have multiple sessions
- stream independently

Each session must be isolated.

Initial configurable maximum:

MAX_ACTIVE_LOG_SESSIONS=20

This must be enforced centrally by the session manager/backend, not merely by the frontend.

When the limit is reached, return a clear user-facing message such as:

"Log streaming capacity reached. 20 active log sessions are currently in use. Terminate an existing session before starting another."

Do not silently queue sessions unless the existing architecture already supports such behavior.

==================================================
TEMPORARY FILE CLEANUP
==================================================

Because sessions are disk-backed, implement/plan for:

1. Cleanup on normal Terminate Session
2. Cleanup of orphaned sessions during application startup
3. Stale-session cleanup as a safety mechanism
4. Protection against abandoned temporary workspaces
5. Safe cleanup so one user's session cannot delete another user's files

Do not assume process shutdown is always graceful.

==================================================
LIVE STREAMING
==================================================

Live streaming is explicitly opt-in.

Inside the log viewer provide controls conceptually similar to:

[ Go Live ] [ Auto Scroll ] [ Stop Live ] [ Terminate Session ]

Go Live:
- opens/follows Kubernetes pod/container logs
- appends incoming logs to the session's temporary log file
- updates the viewer

Stop Live:
- stops the live stream
- preserves existing session logs
- keeps the viewer open

Terminate:
- completely destroys the session and temporary workspace

==================================================
AUTO SCROLL
==================================================

Auto-scroll and live streaming are independent.

Support:

Live OFF + Auto-scroll OFF
→ static historical logs

Live OFF + Auto-scroll ON
→ historical logs positioned at latest entry

Live ON + Auto-scroll OFF
→ new logs arrive but viewport does not jump

Live ON + Auto-scroll ON
→ automatically follow latest logs

If live logs are arriving while auto-scroll is disabled:
- show a small "N new logs" indicator
- allow the user to jump to the latest entry

Do not forcibly move the user's viewport while they are investigating an older log entry.

